<section class="section" id="papers">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow reveal">Papers</span>
    <h2 class="reveal">Tables, retrieval, and teaching small models to reason</h2>
    <p class="lede reveal">
      Each paper asks a narrower question than the last. Can a small model learn the useful part of a bigger model's
      reasoning without copying all of it? Can a table be retrieved no matter how it is written down?
    </p>

    <div class="papers">
      <article class="paper featured reveal">
        <div>
          <div class="venue">
            <span class="pill hot">ICLR 2027, under review</span>
            <span class="pill">First author</span>
          </div>
          <h3>EKLAV: Elicited Key CoT Learning via Answer-Only Supervision</h3>
          <p class="formerly">Previously released as TabRank.</p>
          <p class="body">
            The teacher's reasoning goes into the prompt, and the student trains only on the final answer. On BRIGHT
            passage reranking it wins 41 of 48 model and domain comparisons. Against chain-of-thought distillation, it
            cuts training compute by 24 to 32% and inference time by 15 to 33%.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="side">
          <div class="facts">
            <div class="fact">
              <div class="num"><span class="grad-text">41 / 48</span></div>
              <div class="cap">model and domain comparisons won on BRIGHT</div>
            </div>
            <div class="fact">
              <div class="num"><span class="grad-text">29</span> models</div>
              <div class="cap">and 7 datasets released on Hugging Face</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="links">
            <a class="link arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25182" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="dot"></span>arXiv 2607.25182</a>
            <a class="link" href="https://huggingface.co/AdarshSingh7647" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="dot"></span>Hugging Face models</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="paper reveal">
        <div>
          <div class="venue">
            <span class="pill hot">ACL 2026, main conference</span>
            <span class="pill">First author</span>
          </div>
          <h3>CRAFT: Training-Free Cascaded Retrieval for Tabular QA</h3>
          <p class="body">
            A zero-shot retrieval pipeline. A cheap sparse model filters the candidate tables first, then dense
            rerankers take a closer look. It makes 33x fewer embedding calls and reaches Recall@10 of 87.16 on
            NQ-Tables, with F1 of 67.72 against 54.17.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="side">
          <div class="facts">
            <div class="fact">
              <div class="num"><span class="grad-text">33x</span></div>
              <div class="cap">fewer embedding calls</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="links">
            <a class="link acl" href="https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.149/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="dot"></span>ACL Anthology</a>
            <a class="link arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14984" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="dot"></span>arXiv 2505.14984</a>
            <a class="link gh" href="https://github.com/coral-lab-asu/CRAFT" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="dot"></span>Code</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="paper reveal">
        <div>
          <div class="venue">
            <span class="pill hot">NAACL 2027, submitted</span>
            <span class="pill">Co-first author</span>
          </div>
          <h3>Improving Robustness of Tabular Retrieval via Representational Stability</h3>
          <p class="body">
            Table retrievers change their answers depending on how the same table is written, as CSV, TSV, HTML,
            Markdown or DDL. I treat those formats as noisy views of one meaning and use their centroid as a stable
            target. A small adapter on a frozen encoder learns to move each format toward it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="side">
          <div class="facts">
            <div class="fact">
              <div class="num"><span class="grad-text">5</span> formats</div>
              <div class="cap">serializations tested for the same tables</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="links">
            <a class="link arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24040" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="dot"></span>arXiv 2604.24040</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
